WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.106
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.406 --> 00:00:08.758
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:15.855
Unit nine: Listening: Academic Talks. Today you will follow a short lecture and understand its structure.

4
00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:18.622
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:22.649
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:24.252
Lecture.

7
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.679
Lecture means a formal talk to students.

8
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:29.818
Take notes in the lecture.

9
00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:32.547
Your turn. Say: Lecture.

10
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.459
Lecture.

11
00:00:37.217 --> 00:00:38.032
Topic.

12
00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:40.993
Topic means the subject of a talk.

13
00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:43.467
The topic is climate change.

14
00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:46.736
Your turn. Say: Topic.

15
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.473
Topic.

16
00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.229
Signpost.

17
00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:56.157
Signpost means a phrase that shows structure.

18
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.366
Firstly is a signpost.

19
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.707
Your turn. Say: Signpost.

20
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:05.730
Signpost.

21
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:07.531
Illustrate.

22
00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:11.143
Illustrate means to explain with an example.

23
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.866
The professor illustrates the point.

24
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.917
Your turn. Say: Illustrate.

25
00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:20.768
Illustrate.

26
00:01:21.517 --> 00:01:22.344
Theory.

27
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.058
Theory means an idea that explains something.

28
00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.441
Darwin's theory of evolution.

29
00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:32.352
Your turn. Say: Theory.

30
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:35.957
Theory.

31
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:37.695
Evidence.

32
00:01:38.683 --> 00:01:41.379
Evidence means facts that support an idea.

33
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:43.588
She presents evidence.

34
00:01:44.183 --> 00:01:46.522
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

35
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:50.391
Evidence.

36
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.360
Counterargument.

37
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.895
Counterargument means a point against an idea.

38
00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:58.425
He mentions a counterargument.

39
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:01.750
Your turn. Say: Counterargument.

40
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.880
Counterargument.

41
00:02:06.617 --> 00:02:07.583
Summary.

42
00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:11.245
Summary means a short version of the main points.

43
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.548
The lecture ends with a summary.

44
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.744
Your turn. Say: Summary.

45
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.531
Summary.

46
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:25.449
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:28.503
Visal, what is an academic talk?

48
00:02:29.617 --> 00:02:43.728
It is a short lecture, or a talk by a professor, about a topic such as biology, history or art. Questions ask about the main idea, details and how the talk is organised.

49
00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:46.689
How do I follow the structure?

50
00:02:47.783 --> 00:02:58.551
Listen for signposts like first, for example, however and in summary. They show when the speaker adds an example, changes direction or concludes.

51
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.185
How should I take notes?

52
00:03:02.283 --> 00:03:10.625
Write the topic, then main points and examples in short words. Do not write full sentences, because you will miss the next point.

53
00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:13.186
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:18.070
Grammar focus: Strategy: topic, points, examples.

55
00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:19.487
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:22.914
However signals a change of direction in the talk.

57
00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:26.536
For example introduces evidence for the point.

58
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:34.169
Identify the topic, follow the signposts, and note the main points and examples. Do not write full sentences.

59
00:03:34.917 --> 00:03:38.266
Be careful! Don't say: I miss however in a talk.

60
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.826
Say: I catch however as a signpost.

61
00:03:43.126 --> 00:03:44.511
Signposts matter.

62
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.964
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:50.836
A Lecture on Coral Reefs.

64
00:03:51.136 --> 00:03:54.436
The professor introduces coral reefs as a rich habitat.

65
00:03:55.433 --> 00:03:58.304
Firstly, she explains how corals build reefs.

66
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.497
However, warm water causes bleaching.

67
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.160
For example, a large reef lost half its coral in one summer.

68
00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:10.385
She summarises that protection requires global action.

69
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:15.595
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.066
Signposts show blank.

71
00:04:18.366 --> 00:04:21.811
A, structure. B, spelling. C, maps.

72
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:27.424
The answer is A: structure.

73
00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:30.629
However usually signals blank.

74
00:04:30.929 --> 00:04:35.953
A, a name. B, a change of direction. C, a conclusion only.

75
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.987
The answer is B: a change of direction.

76
00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.614
The professor illustrates a point with blank.

77
00:04:45.914 --> 00:04:49.858
A, a poem. B, a song. C, an example.

78
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.643
The answer is C: an example.

79
00:04:56.783 --> 00:04:58.226
What causes bleaching?

80
00:04:58.526 --> 00:05:03.015
A, warm water. B, cold rain. C, new roads.

81
00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:08.798
The answer is A: warm water.

82
00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.578
Notes should use blank.

83
00:05:11.878 --> 00:05:16.367
A, no words. B, short words. C, full sentences.

84
00:05:19.933 --> 00:05:22.328
The answer is B: short words.

85
00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:26.731
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:27.583 --> 00:05:30.070
How do you take notes in an academic talk?

87
00:05:38.883 --> 00:05:40.268
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:40.568 --> 00:05:53.590
I write the topic at the top and then note the main points and examples in short words. I listen for signposts like however and for example to follow the structure. At the end I write the summary point.

89
00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:55.565
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:56.450 --> 00:06:02.214
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: topic, points, examples.

91
00:06:02.514 --> 00:06:06.490
Today's value is lifelong learning. Keep learning for as long as you live.

92
00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.279
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

93
00:06:09.579 --> 00:06:12.243
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

